**It is not finished as a submission yet.** The core mechanism is strong, but the current repo is underselling the most valuable consequence of the work: **Aletheia is an incremental-computation and cache-invalidation layer for autonomous-agent knowledge**, not merely a “tell me when a research claim got stale” tool.

There are also several concrete release problems right now.

The public repo description says **“Alethiea — agentic verification framework,”** while the README and submission are still branded **Pāṭala Research CI**.   The submission still links judges to the old `prx0r/hackathon1` repository rather than this repo.  And the latest pushed commit is still the August 19 `pitch-verified.md` commit; the hardened release work we produced afterward has evidently not been integrated into GitHub yet. There are also no commit-status checks reported on that HEAD.

More importantly, **the current presentation spends 80% of its attention on mechanism and perhaps 20% on why anyone should care**.

## The actual product is bigger than the current pitch

The README currently says:

> conclusion → dependency → source change → proof obligation.

Accurate. Not very exciting.

The bigger abstraction is:

```text
AUTONOMOUS AGENTS PRODUCE DERIVED STATE

Research
    ↓
analysis
    ↓
claims
    ↓
reports
    ↓
recommendations
    ↓
other agents consume those results
    ↓
more derived state
```

The source world keeps changing.

Without Aletheia you get:

```text
SOURCE UPDATE
     ↓
????

Should I:
- trust everything already computed?
- rerun everything?
- ask another LLM?
