# The economic value is real

There are actually **four costs** Aletheia avoids.

### 1. LLM inference

A research agent doesn't make one LLM call.

Alien describes a harness where a main agent dispatches work to specialist subagents and their internal agents operate over OpenAIRE-scale bibliometric sources. ([Alien Club][1])

So maintaining one conclusion may involve:

```text
query generation
     +
retrieval
     +
relevance classification
     +
paper inspection
     +
citation analysis
     +
subagent synthesis
     +
final synthesis
```

If only one premise changed, rerunning this whole tree is wasteful.

Aletheia changes:

```text
cost of maintenance
≈ cost(original agent run)
```

into:

```text
cost of maintenance
≈ cost(affected derivation subtree)
```

That is a real architecture-level saving.

Alien themselves care enough about inference cost to use Anthropic prompt caching specifically to avoid repaying for repeated context, and their harness includes cost accounting. ([Alien Club][2])

Aletheia applies essentially the same philosophy **one level higher**:

> Don't merely cache prompts. **Cache justified conclusions and invalidate them correctly.**

That's a superb pitch.

---

# 2. MCP/API/data costs

This may become even more important than tokens.

Alien's business is explicitly moving toward **metered agent access to datasets and MCP sources**. Their infrastructure promotes usage-based monetization and tracking each data interaction; their OpenAIRE product positions itself as an enterprise AI gateway. ([Alien Club][3])

So a future research agent might spend money on:

```text
OpenAIRE call
Crossref call
publisher MCP call
premium dataset call
specialist agent call
model call
```

A full rerun re-pays all of them.

Aletheia can say:

```text
this conclusion used:

OpenAIRE A
OpenAIRE B
PremiumSource C
Model execution D

Only B changed.

Do not repurchase A.
Do not repurchase C.
Do not rerun D unless B affects its premise.
```

That gives you a much stronger economic line:

> **Aletheia prevents agents from repurchasing intelligence they have already established.**

That is good.

---

# 3. Human review

This could be the largest saving of all.

Suppose a policy intelligence product has 5,000 derived claims.

A new Graph release occurs.

Without dependencies:

```text
"Something changed."

→ analyst checks whole report
```

With Aletheia:

```text
4,861 claims remain CURRENT

117 deterministic recomputations

19 need model-assisted reassessment

3 need human judgment
```

Now the scarce resource—human expert attention—is routed to **three cruxes**.

That fits OpenAIRE's broader ambitions particularly well because their strategy is explicitly moving toward trustworthy AI-enabled research workflows and research intelligence as accountable infrastructure. ([OpenAIRE][4])

This is not just compute optimization.

It's:

> **attention routing.**

